

PING(8)             UNIX Programmer's Manual              PING(8)



NAME
     ping - send ICMP ECHO_REQUEST packets to network hosts

SYNOPSIS
     ping host [count]

DESCRIPTION
     Ping sends ICMP ECHO_REQUEST packets to host and prints each
     ECHO_REPLY, one line per probe, with the sequence number and
     round-trip time.  The host may be a dotted-quad address or a
     name resolved through /etc/hosts and DNS (see gethostbyname(3)).

     With no count, ping probes once per second until interrupted
     with ^C, then prints a summary.  A count limits ping to that
     many probes before summarizing (useful in scripts).

     Each reply is verified end-to-end: the identifier, sequence
     number, AND the 56-byte payload must be echoed back byte-for-
     byte, so a reply counts only if the remote copied the request
     data verbatim.  The summary reports packets sent and received,
     percent loss, and min/avg/max round-trip time.

     Ping uses a raw socket with the W5100 IPRAW offload; the
     Ethernet chip computes the IP and ICMP checksums.

LIMITATIONS
     Round-trip time is measured with the 60 Hz system tick (16.67
     ms resolution), so a sub-tick reply reads 0 ms; under emulation
     the timing reflects emulated, not physical, network delay.  TTL
     is not reported: the IPRAW receive delivers the ICMP message
     with the IP header (and its TTL) already stripped.

EXAMPLES
     ping www                  probe until ^C (search-domain applied)
     ping 192.168.2.1 4        four probes, then a summary

SEE ALSO
     host(1), ifconfig(8)



